Multiple Sclerosis (MS)
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Dissemination in time and space, Unilateral optic neuritis, Lhermitte's sign/Uhthoff's phenomenon

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-028
Reason for Visit: Acute, painful loss of vision in one eye accompanied by severe electrical shocks down the spine.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, rapidly progressive loss of central vision in the right eye associated with severe pain on eye movement. Urgent in-person evaluation is required to diagnose acute optic neuritis, rule out sight-threatening structural ocular emergencies, and initiate high-dose corticosteroid therapy to preserve optic nerve function before regular hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My right eye hurts terribly when I look around, and there is a huge grey blur right in the center of my vision. Whenever I bend my neck, electricity shoots down my back."
History of Present Illness: The patient, who has a history of transient leg numbness two years ago, reports an acute onset of severe unilateral optic neuritis over the past 48 hours. This is prominently presenting with extremely painful eye movement and central vision loss in the right eye. Today, the patient developed a classic Lhermitte's sign, vividly described as an electric shock-like sensation radiating aggressively down the spine immediately upon passive neck flexion. The patient also reports severe, completely debilitating central fatigue entirely disproportionate to any minor physical exertion over the last week. The patient notes that a hot shower this morning caused a transient worsening of the vision and profound weakness in the legs, characteristic of Uhthoff's phenomenon. There is a prominent neurogenic bladder leading to extreme urinary urgency over the past two days.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently unable to drive or read due to the central scotoma and severe fatigue.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; acute visual impairment risking falls or medication errors.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 76 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert, wearing sunglasses indoors, keeping the neck rigidly straight to avoid sensory shocks.
Physical Exam: Neuro-ophthalmologic examination reveals a marked relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) in the right eye. Visual acuity is severely reduced to 6/60 in the right eye, while the left is 6/6. The patient winces in pain upon testing extraocular movements. Fundoscopy of the right eye reveals a hyperemic, swollen optic disc (papillitis). Neurological examination is positive for Lhermitte's sign upon active neck flexion. Spasticity and profound hyperreflexia prominently affect the lower extremities (3+ reflexes, sustained clonus at the right ankle). The clinical picture confirms distinct, separated neurological deficits rigorously disseminated in both clinical time (leg numbness 2 years ago, optic neuritis today) and anatomical space (spinal cord and optic nerve).
Environmental Observations: Safe environment; hot water bottle found on the couch, which may have exacerbated symptoms.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Multiple Sclerosis - Status: Acute Relapse (Optic Neuritis and Myelitis)
Differentials:

Acute Angle-Closure Glaucoma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Reactive pupil (though with an RAPD), clear cornea, and the pain being distinctly triggered by extraocular muscle movement rather than a constant, severe orbital pressure.

Neuromyelitis Optica (NMO) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clinically indistinguishable from MS at this exact acute presentation, but MS is statistically far more likely; definitive differentiation requires specific aquaporin-4 antibody testing.

Cervical Radiculopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lhermitte's sign radiates down the entire spine rather than a single arm dermatome, and it cannot explain the concurrent acute optic neuritis and central visual loss.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a severe, acute demyelinating flare involving the optic nerve and cervical spinal cord. The acute optic neuritis threatens permanent visual impairment if untreated. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would expose the optic nerve to prolonged, unchecked severe inflammation, increasing the risk of irreversible axonal damage and permanent central blindness. Urgent after-hours assessment was critical to definitively diagnose the optic neuritis via pupillary and visual acuity testing, explicitly rule out acute ophthalmologic surgical emergencies, and immediately initiate high-dose oral or intravenous corticosteroids to rapidly suppress the demyelinating immune cascade, fulfilling MBS criteria for urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed high-dose oral Prednisolone (500mg daily for 5 days) as an acute pulse therapy for the optic neuritis. Arranged an urgent, fast-tracked outpatient MRI of the brain and cervical spine.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By strictly taking cool or lukewarm showers instead of hot baths, the patient actively prevents core body temperature elevation, completely avoiding the paralyzing weakness of Uhthoff's phenomenon.
Step 2: By taking the massive dose of steroids strictly in the morning with a large meal, the patient protects the stomach lining and actively prevents the severe, drug-induced insomnia associated with evening dosing.
Step 3: By scheduling strict, scheduled rest periods every two hours, the patient actively paces their energy, preventing the devastating, complete central fatigue that forces them to bed for days.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to an MS specialist neurology clinic.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Regular GP review in 3 days to monitor steroid tolerance and visual recovery.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop sudden, complete darkness in the eye (no light perception at all), extreme weakness entirely paralyzing a limb, or if you become completely unable to pass urine for more than 8 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The diagnosis of MS requires formal confirmation via MRI and potentially a lumbar puncture.
Clinical Course: The high-dose steroids should rapidly reduce the eye pain within 48 hours and begin to restore vision over the next 1-3 weeks. Complete visual recovery is common but not guaranteed.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for urgent medical review if the steroids cause severe abdominal pain, black tarry stools, or profound, unmanageable manic mood changes.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system is aggressively attacking the insulation (myelin) on your optic nerve and spinal cord. This causes the pain when the eye moves and the electrical shocks in your back. The massive dose of steroids acts as a powerful fire extinguisher, shutting down this immune attack to save the nerve fibers and restore your sight.
Actionable Steps: Start the steroid pulse immediately tomorrow morning with food. Avoid all hot environments. Keep your neck relatively still to avoid the electrical shocks.

